Solution. Let the given consecutive natural numbers be denoted as $k, k+1, \ldots, k+19$. Notice that if some number lies on the segment $[k ; k+19]$, then the sum of the distances from it to the given twenty numbers does not exceed $10 \cdot 19=190$ (the sum of the distances to the two extreme numbers is exactly 19, the sum of the distances to $k+1$ and $k+18$ does not exceed 19, the sum of the distances to $k+2$ and $k+17$ also does not exceed 19, and so on). Therefore, the numbers $a$ and $a^{2}$ lie outside the segment $[k ; k+19]$. Then the sum of the distances from the number $a$ to each of the given consecutive numbers is expressed by the formula $|20 a-k-(k+1)-\ldots-(k+19)|=|20 a-20 k-190|$. Similarly, the sum of the distances from the number $a^{2}$ to each of the given numbers is $\left|20 a^{2}-20 k-190\right|$. We obtain the system of equations

$$
\left\{\begin{array} { l } 
{ | 2 0 a ^ { 2 } - 2 0 k - 1 9 0 | = 2 7 5 5 , } \\
{ | 2 0 a - 2 0 k - 1 9 0 | = 4 4 6 0 . }
\end{array} \Leftrightarrow \left\{\begin{array}{l}
\left|a^{2}-k-9.5\right|=137.75 \\
|a-k-9.5|=223
\end{array}\right.\right.
$$

Consider four cases for the removal of the modulus.

1) Both numbers $a$ and $a^{2}$ lie to the right of the segment $[k ; k+19]$. Then

$$
\left\{\begin{array} { l } 
{ a ^ { 2 } - k - 9 , 5 = 1 3 7 , 7 5 , } \\
{ a - k - 9 , 5 = 2 2 3 }
\end{array} \Leftrightarrow \left\{\begin{array}{l}
a-k-9.5=223 \\
a^{2}-a+85.25=0
\end{array}\right.\right.
