Solution. Let $\sqrt{x+4}-\sqrt{6-x}=t$. Squaring both sides of this equation, we get $(x+4)-2 \sqrt{(x+4)(6-x)}+(6-x)=t^{2}$, from which $2 \sqrt{24+2 x-x^{2}}=10-t^{2}$. The equation becomes $t+4=10-t^{2}$; hence $t^{2}+t-6=0$, i.e., $t=2$ or $t=-3$. We consider each case separately.

$$
